# Copy this file to .env and adjust as needed. .env is git-ignored.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_* variables are inlined into the JS bundle at build time by
# Expo — fine for the client-side Firebase config below (it's designed to be
# public; Firestore rules are the real access control), but it does mean
# never put an actual secret key behind an EXPO_PUBLIC_ prefix.

# Optional cloud sync (see README's "Accounts & cloud sync" section). Leave
# these blank to keep the app fully local-only — Settings → Account & cloud
# sync then shows a clear "not set up" message instead of a broken sign-in
# form. Fill them in with your own Firebase project's web app config
# (Firebase console → Project settings → General → Your apps) to turn
# accounts and cross-device sync on. These are client config values, not
# secrets — see firebaseApp.ts for why that's fine to ship.
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# Google sign-in. Web needs nothing beyond the Firebase config above — it
# signs in via a popup straight against your Firebase project. Native
# (Android/iOS) has no popup API, so it goes through expo-auth-session
# instead, which needs its own Google OAuth web client id: Firebase Console
# → Authentication → Sign-in method → Google → "Web SDK configuration" →
# Web client ID. Leave blank and the Google button on native shows a clear
# "not set up" state rather than a broken one; the iOS/Android client ids
# are optional extras for a smoother native consent screen, not required.
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_ANDROID_CLIENT_ID=
